    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d70">

                <pb facs="0180" n="148" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v02/pg148"/>

                <head>70. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in Vietnam<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d70-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1965-02-04T15:11:00-05:00">February 4, 1965,
                            3:11 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>1608. Instructions going out by military channels to appropriate commands calling
                    off De Soto patrol during and immediately after Kosygin visit to Hanoi. Latest
                    info indicates Kosygin arriving Hanoi February 6 and remaining for approximately
                    four days.</p>
                <p>We feel that having De Soto concurrent with Kosygin visit or immediately
                    thereafter would be seen as reaction to visit. Aside from seriously complicating
                    Moscow-Washington relations, having De Soto patrol at time of Kosygin visit
                    might invite reprisals in such a way as to bring Soviets in.</p>
                <p>Commands will be informed soonest of date for rescheduling patrol.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Ball</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d70-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d70-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, POL 27 VIET S. Secret; Priority;
                    Limdis. Drafted by Green, cleared by Richard Davis (EUR), and approved by
                    William Bundy.</p>
            </note>
